==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
*[[eow|ēow]], [[eoh|ēoh]]

===Etymology===
{{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*īwaz}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*h₁eiH-u̯eh₂-}}, a suffixed form of the base {{m|ine-pro|*h₁eiH}} ‘reddish, colourful’.  Cognate with Old High German [[iwa|īwa]] (German [[Eibe]]), Middle Low German [[iwe|īwe]] (Dutch [[ijf]]), Old Norse {{term|ýr||bow, yew|lang=non}}.  The Indo-European root is perhaps also the source of Old Irish ''ibar'' (Irish [[iúr]]), Baltic ''*eiwa-'' (Lithuanian [[ieva|ievà]]), Slavic ''*jiva'' (Russian [[ива]] ‘willow’), Latin [[uva|ūva]] ‘bunch of grapes’.

The West Germanic languages have a variant {{m|gem-pro|*īhwaz}} with a guttural consonant, represented by Old English [[eoh|ēoh]].

